What is the output of the following Java program demonstrating why immutable classes should be final?
```java
class ImmutablePoint {
    private final int x;
    private final int y;
    public ImmutablePoint(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
    public int getX() { return x; }
    public int getY() { return y; }
}
class MutablePoint extends ImmutablePoint {
    private int mutableX;
    public MutablePoint(int x, int y) { super(x, y); this.mutableX = x; }
    public void setX(int x) { this.mutableX = x; }
    public int getX() { return mutableX; }
}
public class PointApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ImmutablePoint p = new MutablePoint(10, 20);
        ((MutablePoint) p).setX(50);
        System.out.println(p.getX());
    }
}
```

